# Changelog

Every RedstonePanel release, newest first. This file is generated from `debian/changelog` by
`packaging/changelog.py`; edit that instead. The highlights of each release, with how to install
it, are in `release-notes/` and on the releases page: https://github.com/qrussell/redstonepanel/releases

## 1.28.1 (2026-11-28)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.28.1.md

- Cluster: Open dashboard on the master's Cluster page opened a node's sign-in page instead of signing in, since 1.25.0: the master never passed on that the node allows it. It signs in again, and the Dashboard's Open link on a node's card now signs in too. Only the master needs the update.

## 1.28.0 (2026-11-28)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.28.0.md

- Sign in with Discord: a Sign in with Discord button on the sign-in page (Panel Settings > Sign in with Discord: client ID and secret of your Discord application, the redirect addresses, your Discord server). Admins and helpers link their own account and keep their two-factor; people without an account sign in on their Discord roles, each role giving chosen permissions on chosen servers, never admin. Roles are checked again every 10 minutes through the /start bot. Accounts can be blocked.
- Join pages: Who can see this page. Anyone with the link (as before), anyone who signs in, or by permission: who may see how to join, Start and Stop, by panel account, permission, Discord membership or role, or visitors who aren't signed in (see and Start only). A page can require joining your Discord server and show its invite.
- Whitelist by Discord: players link their Minecraft name with a button the bot posts or /minecraft, and the panel keeps each server's whitelist (Bedrock: allowlist): per server Off, anyone in your Discord, chosen roles, or moderator approval. Moderators approve new people in a Discord channel or the panel, which can also give a role; trusted invites skip that. Losing the role, leaving or being blocked takes the name off. Names added by hand are never touched. Clusters: the master keeps its nodes' whitelists.
- Start only: a per-server permission to start a server but not stop it, for accounts, API tokens and Discord roles.
- Discord /start: the Discord server's owner and administrators may use it without a listed role. If Discord refuses the Server Members Intent, the bot runs without it and the panel says what to turn on.

## 1.27.0 (2026-11-27)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.27.0.md

- Discord /start: people in your Discord type /start, pick a server, and the panel starts it (or wakes it from sleep mode), then edits its answer to say when the server is online and where to join. The panel itself is the bot: Panel Settings > Discord takes a bot token from the Discord Developer Portal, the Discord servers it answers in, optionally the roles that may use it, and the game servers it may start. It connects out to Discord, so nothing is forwarded, on Linux and Windows. It only ever starts servers: one start per server every 2 minutes and 5 per person an hour, logged as discord:NAME.

## 1.26.0 (2026-11-26)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.26.0.md

- Sleep mode, per server on the Maintenance tab (and `redstonepanel sleep NAME on [MINUTES]|off`): after the minutes you choose with nobody on, the panel stops the server and answers on its port. Java shows "Sleeping: join to start it" in the server list; joining starts it and the player joins again about 30 seconds later. Bedrock wakes on the first try to join. Works on Linux, Windows and cluster nodes, and is kept through moves. A sleeping server never starts at boot: after a reboot it goes back to sleep if Start at boot is on.
- Join pages: "Let visitors start the server" adds a Start the server button to a stopped server's public page (off by default; one start every 2 minutes per server, 3 an hour per visitor).
- Port check: right after a server started, the dashboard could say its port was held by a program the system can't name, for up to three minutes. It now looks again before saying so.

## 1.25.0 (2026-11-26)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.25.0.md

- API tokens for scripts and bots: Panel Settings > API tokens, or `redstonepanel token create`. A token may do only what you tick, on the servers you choose (cluster nodes' servers too), like a helper account. It is never an admin, is shown once, expires, can be revoked, and every change it makes is logged under its name.
- Start at boot, per server, on the Maintenance tab (and `redstonepanel boot NAME on|off`). Off, a server stays stopped after a reboot until someone starts it. Kept through restores, reinstalls and moves.
- Open dashboard, signed in: an admin on the master opens a node's own panel already signed in, with no account there. Each node can turn it off under Settings > Cluster.
- Windows: Update now on the dashboard installs the update again. It downloaded the installer but never started it. Panels on 1.24.x need this update installed once by hand.

## 1.24.1 (2026-11-26)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.24.1.md

- Windows: creating, joining or leaving a cluster opens or closes the panel's HTTPS port in its firewall rule. A Windows PC that joined on 1.24.0 only had port 8080 open, so its master couldn't reach it; the panel now checks the rule at every start and fixes it.
- A server created on a node from the master shows in the server list straight away, without reloading the page.
- A cluster master's Dashboard and Cluster page load much faster: each member no longer runs the full port check whenever it's asked for its figures (it still runs every two minutes), and the page draws the members first.

## 1.24.0 (2026-11-26)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.24.0.md

- Mixed Linux and Windows clusters: a Windows PC can be a cluster's master or a node next to Linux machines. The Cluster page and Panel Settings say Linux or Windows for each member.
- Java and Bedrock servers move both ways; a Bedrock server gets the new machine's own program. A move or restore onto Windows is refused, with the list, when the server holds file names Windows can't.
- Backup copies, restores and encrypted cluster backups work across systems.
- SFTP on Windows, and HTTPS with your own certificate (win-acme or certbot) picks up a renewed certificate by itself. Windows is no longer a preview.
- Windows sign-in: Windows Hello (face, fingerprint or PIN) or a phone through passkeys, added under Password; it counts as two-factor sign-in.
- The Windows installer's Finish page opens a one-use link to choose the admin password, and the Start menu gets RedstonePanel.
- Remember this device: a 30-day sign-in on your own computer or phone, listed under Password with Forget.
- `redstonepanel reset-password NAME` sets a forgotten panel password.
- A server moved away and back keeps its name; start, stop and restart on a node wait for its answer.

## 1.23.2 (2026-11-25)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.23.2.md

- Panel Settings > Remove RedstonePanel and `sudo redstonepanel uninstall` remove the panel safely: back up every server, choose for each one (stop it and keep its files, keep it running without the panel, or delete it after a recent backup), then remove the package.
- The Maintenance tab links to it, and the message after installing and the Windows installer's last page say how to remove the panel.
- Removing the package never stops a game server nobody chose for: it turns off the panel's own timers and services and lists every server left running or starting at boot, with the command to stop it.
- Installing the panel again turns those servers' backup and restart schedules back on.

## 1.23.1 (2026-11-25)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.23.1.md

- Port check: the Port forwarding page compares every port the servers and the panel need with what really uses each port, and names the other program (process, path, command line, service or Docker container). The panel never stops or changes another program.
- RCON, Query and mod ports another program holds move by themselves; a game port moves to a free one on a click, with the firewall opened.
- Starting a server whose game port another program holds is refused with the reason, instead of crash-looping.
- Check the router reads the router's forwards over UPnP and names the ones that point to the wrong machine or port.
- Needs attention, the Last crash card, `redstonepanel ports` and a new port_conflict webhook report port problems. Linux and Windows.
- A bind probe finds ports held where the socket lists can't see, such as WSL mirrored networking on Windows.

## 1.23.0 (2026-11-25)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.23.0.md

- Windows: Bedrock servers (Mojang's Windows build) with automatic updates, and Phantom for LAN play.
- Windows: Quilt, Forge and NeoForge servers and modpacks; their installers run as the new server's own account.
- Windows: hard memory and processor limits (Job Objects), performance history, and crash explanations with Windows causes (a reserved port, a locked file, a missing Visual C++ runtime, the memory limit).
- Windows: off-site backup copies to SSH storage (OpenSSH's sftp) and S3.
- Windows: Update now updates the panel from its GitHub release, SHA-256 checked; game servers stop for about a minute and start again.
- Windows: sign in with a Windows account in Administrators or a local RedstonePanel group; one spelling per account.
- Windows: the Port forwarding page knows the router, and Needs attention names ports Hyper-V, WSL or Docker reserved, a PC set to sleep and a pending Windows Update restart.
- Windows: the panel's firewall rule also opens the HTTPS port when HTTPS is on.
- The terminal stays Linux-only by design; clusters and SFTP come to Windows in 1.24.
- A new Bedrock server skips 19132/19133 when another program holds them, and Geyser's port is read on NeoForge and BungeeCord too.

## 1.22.0 (2026-11-24)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.22.0.md

- Windows preview: a Windows installer (RedstonePanel-1.22.0-x64.msi) for Windows 11 and Windows Server 2022 or 2025, 64-bit. Python comes inside it; Java (Eclipse Temurin) is downloaded when a server needs it.
- On Windows the preview runs Java servers (Vanilla, Paper, Purpur, Fabric) on one computer, with backups and restore, schedules, the console, Live Chat, the file manager, add-ons, the firewall, performance, crash explanations, webhooks, two-factor sign-in, join pages and a servers folder on any drive. Not yet on Windows: Bedrock, Quilt, Forge, NeoForge, modpacks, limits, off-site storage, SFTP, the terminal, clusters and updating from the panel; the panel leaves them out there.
- Each game server on Windows is its own Windows service running as its own account, schedules are Task Scheduler tasks and ports are Windows Defender Firewall rules.
- Found on this computer always shows for an admin: what it can take over, what it saw and can't manage with the reason, or that nothing was found.
- A new server takes the lowest free port: a removed server whose files were kept no longer holds its port (restoring it moves it to a free one if needed), and the port no longer goes up with the name.

## 1.21.10 (2026-11-24)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.21.10.md

- Terminal: admins can open a shell on the machine from the browser (Dashboard, and each machine on the Cluster page), on a phone too, with a row of extra keys. Linux only, HTTPS only, off until an admin turns it on under Panel Settings -> Terminal.
- The shell always runs as a Linux account, never as the panel: your own account if you signed in to the panel with it, otherwise the account you log in with at its login: prompt. root only when allowed; accounts such as minecraft that can't log in, never. User accounts get no terminal.
- Passwords are checked through PAM, with the panel's two-factor code when it is on. Five wrong passwords in 10 minutes block for 10 minutes.
- Closes after 15 minutes with nothing typed, after 12 hours and on sign-out. 3 per admin, 10 per machine; Open now lists them with End. Every open, close and failed login is logged; what is typed is not.
- Clusters: a node allows a terminal from the master only when its own admin turns it on. Cluster API 8.
- Command line: redstonepanel terminal on|off.
- Signing out now also stops that sign-in opening terminals.
- New dependency: python3-websockets.

## 1.21.9 (2026-11-24)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.21.9.md

- Join pages: Through a proxy (HAProxy or Cloudflare). HAProxy, for example on pfSense, or a Cloudflare Tunnel can send /join on your own domain to the join pages. Enter the proxy's address and each player keeps their own request limit; anyone else's forwarding header is ignored.
- Address players open: the panel's links use your own domain.
- Cloudflare's proxy (orange cloud): requests from Cloudflare's own addresses may name the player. The list ships with the panel and is refreshed from cloudflare.com once a day.
- Only through the proxy: the join port answers nobody but the proxy.
- HAProxy and Cloudflare Tunnel settings to copy, filled in.
- Mods and plugins with a port of their own (Simple Voice Chat, Geyser, BlueMap, Dynmap, Plan, NuVotifier): when two servers share one, the panel moves one to a free port, opens it in the firewall and says so. A crash caused by a taken mod port names that port and mod.
- Upgrades leave game servers running: Ubuntu's needrestart is told not to restart Minecraft servers and their Bedrock proxies after apt upgrades something.
- Fixed: a Last crash card closes on its own once the server has run for 10 minutes after the problem is fixed.
- Fixed: an account added while the panel was starting got every server.
- Fixed: a server whose service runs as another account (such as root) had its files refused since 1.21.8.

## 1.21.8 (2026-11-24)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.21.8.md

- Security: a Bedrock player's name on Configuration > Players is checked before it can reach the server's console, and the panel's Xbox ID fallback runs the server as the minecraft account, not root.
- Security: the panel works in server folders with the minecraft account's rights, so a link the server or a plugin puts there can't make it read or change other files (settings, operators, whitelist, downloads, world rename, Live Chat, installs, updates, restores). Downloads leave links out. On first start, files the panel wrote in server folders as root are handed to the minecraft account.
- Security: a world outside the server folder is never downloaded, renamed or restored, and unknown server names are refused up front.
- Fixed: Java players kept their UUID on Configuration > Players.

## 1.21.7 (2026-11-24)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.21.7.md

- Files tab: a file that is a list of name=value settings (server.properties, eula.txt, most .properties, .cfg, .conf and .ini files) opens with a Visual Editor like Configuration's, with True/False lists, number boxes and a filter. Visual Editor and Raw Text switch between the form and the text and always show the same file.
- The form changes only the values you change: comments, blank lines, spacing and order stay as they were.
- Fixed: saving from the Files tab turned Windows line endings (CRLF) into LF, for example in Bedrock's server.properties.

## 1.21.6 (2026-11-24)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.21.6.md

- Player packs: for a Fabric, Quilt, Forge or NeoForge server the panel works out which mods players need from the server's own mods folder and offers them on the join page as a pack (.mrpack) their launcher installs in one go. Add, update or remove a mod and the pack follows.
- Mods that run only on the server are left out, mods that work without are marked optional, and what a required mod depends on is required. A mod Modrinth doesn't know is named on the page.
- The Mods tab gets a Player pack view: overrule any line, add extras for players such as shaders, name the pack, or turn it off.
- The same pack as a packwiz folder, for launchers that check the server's mods every time the game starts.
- On a cluster master the join page offers the pack of a server on any node.

## 1.21.5 (2026-11-23)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.21.5.md

- Join pages: each server can have a public page that tells players how to join, with the address, whether it's online, how many are playing and the Minecraft version. Off until an admin turns it on.
- A server made from a Modrinth modpack links to the exact pack version it runs, with steps for Modrinth App and Prism Launcher. Bedrock servers and Java servers with Geyser get an Add server button.
- The pages have a port of their own (8444) that answers nothing but the join pages, so the panel itself can stay off the internet.
- Pages can be unlisted (a random link), carry a note and a link of your own, and show player names only if you turn that on.
- On a cluster master the pages cover the servers on every node.

## 1.21.4 (2026-11-23)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.21.4.md

- Live Chat suggests as you type, like the game: type / for the commands that match, then the players online, players seen before or the fixed choices for each part of the command. Tab fills one in.
- A Commands list above the message box opens a form that asks only for what the picked command needs (a player, a choice, a place) and shows the exact command before Run.
- One-click buttons for List players, Save world, Day, Night and Clear weather.
- A running Vanilla, Fabric, Quilt, Forge or NeoForge server tells the panel its own commands and game rules, so commands added by mods are suggested and game rules are named as that Minecraft version has them.
- Java and Bedrock each get their own commands; Bedrock names with spaces are quoted for you. For anyone with Console & chat.

## 1.21.3 (2026-11-23)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.21.3.md

- Found on this computer: New server now lists Minecraft servers on the machine that were set up outside the panel (your own systemd service, a script, a screen session), with folder, port, version and what started each one. Looking changes nothing.
- Manage with RedstonePanel hands one over: the service that ran it is stopped and turned off (never deleted) and the panel runs the server from the same folder under its own service, with the memory it had. No file is copied or moved. A server that doesn't start under the panel is put back by itself.
- Undo puts a handed-over server back as it was.
- Fixed: a new server given the name of one you removed no longer opens with the removed server's Last crash card.

## 1.21.2 (2026-11-23)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.21.2.md

- Light and dark mode is now a sun/moon switch in the top right corner of every page, one click, on the sign-in page too. On a phone it is at the right end of the top bar.
- The accent color is a colored dot beside the switch: Redstone Red, Diamond Blue, Emerald Green or Amethyst Purple, one click each.
- The gear button in the sidebar is gone. It opened the appearance choices under the title Panel Settings and was easy to mistake for the real Settings button at the bottom of the sidebar.
- Your saved mode and color carry over.

## 1.21.1 (2026-11-23)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.21.1.md

- Choose where servers are kept: Panel Settings > Servers folder takes the full path of a folder on any disk or partition, for example /srv/minecraft or /mnt/disk2/minecraft. The panel makes the folder and shows the free space there. Leave it empty for the usual place, /opt.
- New servers go there: created, imported, restored from backup storage, installed from a modpack or moved in from another cluster member. Servers you already have stay where they are and keep working.
- Every folder that was used before is remembered, so names and ports stay unique across them and removed servers are still found.
- Folders the panel can't safely use are refused with the reason: system folders, the top folder of a disk, names with spaces, a read-only or noexec disk, and a folder the minecraft account can't get into.

## 1.21.0 (2026-11-23)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.21.0.md

- Groundwork for the Windows version (roadmap 1.21 to 1.24). Nothing changes on screen and there is nothing to do after upgrading.
- Everything the panel asks of the operating system (systemd, ufw, screen, apt and dpkg, openssl, and where files live) now goes through one internal package, core/platform, so a Windows side can be added next to the Linux one. The panel runs the same commands with the same arguments as before.
- A test now fails the build when panel code outside that package starts a system program or spells out a system path, and a pretend system lets new tests run without touching the machine.

## 1.20.0 (2026-11-23)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.20.0.md

- New Java server types: Purpur (plugins), Quilt, NeoForge and Forge (mods), next to Vanilla, Paper and Fabric. Forge from Minecraft 1.12.2, Quilt from 1.14.4, NeoForge from 1.20.2. Quilt, NeoForge and Forge are installed by their own installer, run as the minecraft user; old Forge versions get the old Java they need.
- New server, From a modpack (admins): search Modrinth's modpacks, or upload a .mrpack or a CurseForge server pack. The panel installs the server type and Minecraft version the pack needs and only its server-side files, each checked against the pack's checksum. Nothing from a pack is run.
- Maintenance, Modpack: check Modrinth for a newer version of the pack or upload one. A backup is taken first, and the world, settings and mods you added yourself are kept.
- Mods and the catalog follow the server's type: Forge mods on Forge, NeoForge mods on NeoForge, Quilt and Fabric mods on Quilt, Paper's plugins on Purpur. A jar for another loader is refused with the reason.
- Server type (1.19) changes to and from the new types. Paper and Purpur, and Fabric and Quilt, keep their plugins and mods in place.
- Update alerts for Purpur, Quilt, NeoForge and Forge builds.
- Fixed: Java 8 from Ubuntu's package was never found as installed.
- In a cluster, nodes need 1.20 for the new types and modpacks.

## 1.19.0 (2026-11-23)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.19.0.md

- Change a Java server's type in place: Maintenance, Server type moves a server between Vanilla, Paper and Fabric (and to a newer Minecraft version at the same time) and keeps its world, port, settings, players and schedules. A preview lists everything that will happen first.
- Always a backup first ("Before type change"), and the old type is put back by itself if the new one doesn't finish starting. Undo type change returns to the old software and the world from before.
- The world's layout follows the type: Paper's separate Nether and End folders (before Minecraft 26.1), and the world settings files Paper keeps with each dimension (26.1 and later), are moved when leaving or joining Paper.
- Mods and plugins are set aside (mods.off, plugins.off) and come back when you return. The same Modrinth project's build for the new type is offered; Fabric API is added when a Fabric mod is installed that way. Leaving Fabric with mods that add blocks, items or dimensions asks for the server's name first, and lists them.
- Update Server (Java) now takes a backup first, and Roll back the last update returns to the server software and world from before it.
- Backups record the type and version that made them (the Made by column), and a world from a newer Minecraft version is not restored onto an older server.
- Changing the type, undoing it and rolling back an update are for admins; seeing a server's type needs View.

## 1.18.0 (2026-11-22)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.18.0.md

- Per-server permissions for user accounts: Users, Servers sets what a helper may do on each server (View, Console & chat, Start/stop/restart, Players, Files, Mods & plugins, Settings, Backups), with Moderator and Co-owner presets and an Every server row. Users see only their servers on the dashboard, in the list, under Needs attention and in the activity log. On a cluster the master keeps and enforces what its users may do on node servers.
- User accounts that already exist keep what users could always do (everything except Files and Mods & plugins) on every server. New user accounts start with no servers.
- Importing a server, the removed-servers list and restore, editing and deleting templates and the cluster's list of moves are for admins only.
- SFTP for FileZilla, WinSCP or any SFTP program, off until turned on under Panel Settings, SFTP (port 2022). Sign in as ACCOUNT.SERVER with the panel or Linux password, or an SSH key added under Password, SSH keys; accounts with two-factor sign-in must use a key. Each sign-in reaches one server's folder, with the file manager's safety checks and no shell. Users need Files on the server; admins reach every server. Runs as redstonepanel-sftp.service and needs python3-paramiko (a new Recommends). Each cluster member serves SFTP for its own servers.

## 1.17.2 (2026-11-22)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.17.2.md

- Advanced options on New server: choose the name and game port, a Paper build or Fabric loader, Vanilla snapshots and pre-releases, the Java to run on, the heap, hard memory and CPU limits, startup flags (Aikar's flags, plus extra -XX/-D/-Xss flags for admins), the world (seed, type, folder name) and common settings (MOTD, game mode, difficulty, max players, online mode, whitelist, operators). Every field is optional, and every choice is checked before anything is downloaded.
- Maintenance, Java card: Startup flags can be changed later. They are kept through updates, Java changes, restores and moves.
- Clusters: nodes need RedstonePanel 1.17.2 for Advanced options (cluster contract 6).

## 1.17.1 (2026-11-22)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.17.1.md

- Why did it crash? When a server crashes or won't start, a Last crash card on its Overview names the cause in plain words (missing dependency, mod or plugin for another version, wrong Java, port in use, out of memory, EULA, damaged world) and the likely mod or plugin, with the log's error lines highlighted.
- One-click fixes on the card, for admins: Turn off and restart the suspected mod or plugin, and Undo the last mod update when there was a recent one. Nothing changes without a click.
- The crash webhook says why, and Needs attention lists the crash with a link to the card.
- Fixed: a server that died within a second of starting (the wrong Java, say) was reported as "stopped" instead of "crashed".
- The RedstonePanel logo and name open the project website, redstonepanel.cielocloud.org.

## 1.17.0 (2026-11-22)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.17.0.md

- Performance on each server's Overview: tick health (TPS and MSPT for Paper, Vanilla and Fabric 1.20.3+), memory against its heap and limit, players and connections, world size, and Held back by its limits when a limit is biting.
- History charts: 24 hours at 1-minute detail and 7 days at 15-minute detail for each server (CPU, memory, players, TPS) and the machine (CPU, memory, disk, network), kept in small fixed-size files with no database. The dashboard's server list gains a TPS column.
- Maintenance > Memory and limits: change a Java server's heap safely, and set optional hard memory and CPU limits per server, applied live through a systemd drop-in.
- Suggestions based on what was measured, never applied automatically, and optional tps_low and disk_full webhook alerts (off by default, at most once an hour).
- Raspberry Pi: on machines with 3 GB or less the heap check keeps 768 MB for the system, and the Pi instructions now point to Memory and limits.

## 1.16.14 (2026-11-22)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.16.14.md

- Port forwarding (admins): a page listing what to forward on your internet router for every server, and on a cluster master for every node, with each machine's LAN address, the protocol and what each port is for. RCON, LAN discovery, Phantom and the panel itself are marked leave closed.
- Step-by-step for pfSense and other routers, a warning when two servers want the same outside port, Check from here, and the internet address asked of your own router (NAT-PMP or UPnP), never an outside website.
- The phone menu scrolls as one page, so the account buttons no longer cover the last servers on short phones.

## 1.16.13 (2026-11-21)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.16.13.md

- Pre-copy while it runs: an option in the Move window copies a server's folder while it keeps running, then stops it and sends only what changed, so a big world is down for seconds instead of minutes. The window says how long the server was down.
- Find panels on your network: Panel Settings > Cluster > Add a node lists the RedstonePanels on the master's private networks, marked as standalone, a node here or in another cluster, with Add to cluster making a join code.
- Cluster contract 5: a pre-copy needs 1.16.13 or later on both members; plain moves still work with older nodes.

## 1.16.12 (2026-11-21)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.16.12.md

- Update available: the panel checks every 6 hours for a newer Bedrock release, Minecraft release, Paper build or Fabric loader (never snapshots), shows it in the sidebar, dashboard, cluster page and Overview, and can send one update_available webhook per version.
- Update automatically for Bedrock servers (off by default): players are warned for 5 minutes, a Before update backup is taken, the files are swapped keeping settings and worlds, and a server that doesn't start goes back to the old version and shows under Needs attention.
- The update check is one machine timer, redstonepanel-updates.timer, so it runs while the panel is stopped. The Update button uses the same safe path.
- Bedrock servers save before they stop: every stop, from the buttons, backups, scheduled restarts, updates or shutting down, sends Bedrock its own stop command and waits for it to quit.

## 1.16.11 (2026-11-21)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.16.11.md

- Off-site backup copies: Panel Settings > Backup storage adds SSH servers (rsync, the panel's own key) and S3-compatible storage (Backblaze B2, Wasabi, Cloudflare R2, MinIO, AWS S3). A server's Backups tab picks where its backups are copied; each copy is checked with SHA-256.
- Copies run after each backup, even while the panel is stopped, and a failed copy shows under Needs attention and sends one backup_copy_failed webhook until it works again.
- Retention by age: backup storage keeps its own count and age, and local schedules can also delete backups after N days. The newest one is never deleted by age.
- Restore from an off-site copy, or bring a server back on a new machine with New Server > From backup storage.
- Bedrock firewall: servers without a transport line (NetherNet, the default since Bedrock 1.26.50) now get TCP server-port and UDP 7551 opened. Existing Bedrock servers are fixed once after the upgrade.
- The panel keeps ufw in step with server.properties edited by hand and with servers removed by hand, and notes game-port rules nothing uses.

## 1.16.10 (2026-11-21)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.16.10.md

- Works on a phone: below 768 px the sidebar becomes a menu under a top bar, Start, Restart and Stop are large buttons, tabs scroll sideways, tables become cards and dialogs fill the screen.
- Fields no longer make iOS zoom in, Live Chat stays above the on-screen keyboard, and touch screens get 44 px controls.
- Installable as an app over HTTPS (a manifest and a service worker that caches nothing but shows a "Can't reach the panel" page when offline).
- Open on your phone: a QR code of the panel's address on the desktop, made by the panel itself, offering the machine's network addresses when the panel was opened as localhost.

## 1.16.9 (2026-11-21)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.16.9.md

- Runs on Ubuntu 24.04, 26.04 and 26.10, Debian 13 and 64-bit Raspberry Pi OS (Pi 4 or 5), on x86-64 and ARM64. Ubuntu 25.04 and 25.10 still install with an end-of-support note; Debian 12 and 32-bit systems are refused with what to install instead.
- Java versions come from what the system packages (Debian 13 has 21 and 25). A Minecraft version needing a Java the system lacks gets the closest newer one, with a note; installing Java 16 for Minecraft 1.17 no longer fails.
- Bedrock is x86-64 only: on ARM64 machines and nodes it is greyed out, and creating, importing, restoring, updating or moving one there is refused.
- New Java servers get a 1 GB heap on machines with 3 GB of memory or less.

## 1.16.8 (2026-11-21)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.16.8.md

- Backup copies on another cluster member: on a cluster master, a server's Backups tab can keep a copy of each backup on the master or another node. The master copies new backups within a few minutes and checks each with SHA-256.
- Scheduled copies follow the server's Keep setting; manual copies stay until deleted. Copies can be downloaded, or put back into the server's backups and restored, even after the original machine is gone.
- A failing copy shows under Needs attention on the master and sends the new backup_copy_failed webhook event.
- Cluster contract 4: every member needs 1.16.8 or later for copies.

## 1.16.7 (2026-11-21)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.16.7.md

- Two-factor sign-in: any panel or Linux account can ask for a six-digit code from an authenticator app after its password. Set it up under Password with a QR code; ten one-use recovery codes are shown once.
- Panel Settings > Sign-in > Require two-factor for admins (off by default): an admin without it can only set it up or sign out.
- Turning two-factor on signs out the account's other sessions. Wrong codes count towards the sign-in rate limit.
- Admins can reset another panel account's two-factor under Users, and root can run redstonepanel reset-2fa NAME for any account.

## 1.16.6 (2026-11-21)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.16.6.md

- The console no longer covers the page. While it's open, the page beside the sidebar ends above it, so buttons near the bottom of a tab can still be scrolled into view, and it starts at the sidebar's edge, so the sidebar's buttons stay visible.
- The console is at most 35% of the window's height (300px on taller windows), and when a click opens it, the button that was clicked is scrolled back into view above it.

## 1.16.5 (2026-11-21)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.16.5.md

- Each new Bedrock server gets its own gameplay port: server-udp-ports is set to the server's port. Since 1.16.4 every new Bedrock server kept the 19132 from Mojang's server.properties, so a second Bedrock server on the same machine clashed with the first.
- Moving, importing or restoring a Bedrock server moves a matching server-udp-ports along with its port, applying a template keeps the server's own, and ports in server-udp-ports count as taken when the panel picks ports for a new server.

## 1.16.4 (2026-11-21)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.16.4.md

- New Bedrock servers keep the full server.properties that comes with the Bedrock download; only the ports are set. Before, every setting except server-port, server-portv6 and query.port was dropped, so Configuration showed almost nothing to edit.

## 1.16.3 (2026-11-21)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.16.3.md

- Server list: every heading folds with a click (this machine's Java and Bedrock Edition, each cluster node), and a folded heading shows how many of its servers are online. What's folded is remembered in the browser.
- A cluster node's servers get their own Java Edition and Bedrock Edition headings, like this machine's. A node's warnings stay visible while it is folded.
- Server names in the server list are HTML-escaped.

## 1.16.2 (2026-11-21)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.16.2.md

- Kick, ban and unban from Whitelist & Access: Online now lists the players on the server with Kick (and Ban on Java), a Banned list with Unban and a form to ban by name, Unban on players turned away for a ban, and Kick/Ban on each access log row. A running server is told through its console (Java over RCON, Bedrock through screen); a stopped Java server's banned-players.json is edited directly. Bedrock has no ban list, so it gets kick only. Bans are by name and UUID, never by address. Each action is logged with who did it.
- GET /api/servers/{name}/moderation and POST /api/servers/{name}/players/kick|ban|unban, reachable by the cluster master.
- A new look: an original RedstonePanel logo and favicon, warm stone greys with redstone-red accents in both the dark and light themes, glowing focus rings, status lamps and usage bars. Redstone Red is the default accent palette; a palette you picked before is kept.
- Stop is an outlined red button, so it no longer looks like Restart.
- Packaging: build-deb.sh no longer strips carriage returns from binary files, which corrupted PNG images.

## 1.16.1 (2026-11-20)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.16.1.md

- First-run setup guide: a Set up RedstonePanel card at the top of the dashboard (admins) with four steps: create your first server, give every server a backup schedule, choose HTTPS or keep plain HTTP on a network you trust, and send crash and backup alerts to a webhook. Each step opens the dialog that already does the job.
- Steps are ticked from the panel's real state, so work done from the command line or by another admin counts, and an upgraded panel only shows what's still missing. The card says when ufw is missing or off, since the panel opens game ports only through it.
- Hide guide hides it for every admin; Panel Settings > Setup guide shows it again, and takes back "keep HTTP". Only those two answers are stored, in setup.json.
- On a cluster node joining the cluster ticks the HTTPS step; a master counts its nodes' servers.
- GET/PUT /api/settings/setup (admins).

## 1.16.0 (2026-11-20)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.16.0.md

- Move a server to another cluster member: on the master's cluster page, admins get Move and Copy for every server. A move stops the server, packs its whole folder, streams it through the master to the other member (checked against its SHA-256, no size cap), installs it there under the same name and ports when they're free, installs the Java it needs, carries over its memory, backup and restart schedules and Phantom, starts it and checks it keeps running. Only then does the old member take it off the panel, keeping its files as a removed server marked "moved to", until an admin deletes them. Any failure undoes the new copy and starts the server where it was.
- Copy a server, to another member or the same one, without stopping it (the world is saved first, as backups do), under a new name where needed, started or not.
- Templates between members: copy one of the master's templates to a node, or a node's own template to the master.
- While a server is being moved it can't be started on the old member.
- Cluster contract 3: nodes accept /api/cluster/transfers/... from their master (a master admin only, never through the proxy). The only files a master can delete on a node are those a move left behind.
- Template names are escaped in the template manager.

## 1.15.0 (2026-11-20)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.15.0.md

- Clusters phase 2: manage every node's servers from the master. The master's server list shows each node's servers under the node's name, and opening one shows every tab: Overview, Live Chat, Configuration (with Whitelist & Access), Maintenance, Backups, World & Maps, Files, Mods, Java and Phantom. Requests go through the master's proxy, /api/cluster/nodes/{id}/proxy/..., with the node's token; downloads and uploads are streamed.
- Nodes accept a wider allowlist from the master: every route of a single server, creating, importing, removing and restoring servers, templates pushed by the master and Java runtimes. Never users, panel settings, HTTPS, sign-in, panel updates, cluster membership changes by proxy, or erasing a server's files.
- Master admins act as admins on the nodes; master users get a user's rights there. Both panels log each change as "[CLUSTER] user@master: ...".
- New Server has a Where choice on a master. A template is copied to the node first, ports are chosen on the node, and a missing Java can be installed there. Save as Template on a node's server saves it as one of the master's templates.
- A cluster master can use its own certificate (certbot or uploaded, HTTPS mode "custom") for browsers; nodes keep cluster certificates on private addresses. A node with its own public certificate can join and is checked with the system's trust store (verify "system").
- The master renews node cluster certificates automatically 30 days before they expire, and its own.
- Nightly cluster backups: Panel Settings > Cluster > Nightly backups (or "redstonepanel cluster nightly on") writes an encrypted backup every night after 3:00, keeping 7.
- The cluster contract goes to version 2. 1.14 nodes still join and show on the Cluster page; update them to manage their servers.

## 1.14.2 (2026-11-20)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.14.2.md

- Player access log: the panel reads each server's console and logs and keeps who joined, left or was turned away (not whitelisted, banned, wrong version, server full), with names and UUIDs/XUIDs only; player addresses are never stored. The first scan reads older logs, so last-seen dates go back as far as the logs do. Events are kept 90 days.
- Configuration -> Whitelist & Access: see and edit the whitelist (Java) or allowlist (Bedrock), with each player's last join. A running server is changed through its console (RCON or screen), so changes apply at once; otherwise the file is edited. Players turned away in the last 7 days are listed with an Allow button, and the access log can be filtered by player, event and period.
- Needs attention lists players turned away in the last 24 hours who still aren't allowed.
- New webhook events, off by default: Player joined, Player turned away.

## 1.14.1 (2026-11-20)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.14.1.md

- Firewall rules follow each server's network settings: Bedrock servers using the NetherNet transport get TCP server-port (signaling), UDP server-udp-ports (gameplay) and UDP 7551 (LAN discovery, shared by all NetherNet servers) instead of UDP server-port. Changing the port or transport in Configuration, or applying a template, opens the new rules and closes the old ones; removing a server closes only rules no other server needs.
- The dashboard checks every rule a server needs and says which are missing, with the ufw command to open them; a closed 7551 is reported as consoles not finding the server under LAN Games.
- Phantom refuses NetherNet servers and says why: it only relays RakNet, and NetherNet servers announce themselves on the LAN by themselves. A NetherNet server on port 19132 no longer stops Phantoms for RakNet servers (its 19132 is TCP), and proxy ports skip its UDP ports.

## 1.14.0 (2026-11-20)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.14.0.md

- A catalog of mods and plugins: Find mods / Find plugins opens on bundles and a hand-picked starter set (performance, admin tools, for players, libraries), checked against the server's loader and Minecraft version, with Installed / Update available / Not for this version badges.
- Bundles install several at once, with what they need, after an optional world backup: Performance pack, Server essentials and Bedrock players on Fabric; Server essentials, Build protection and Bedrock players on Paper.
- Search filters by category and sorts by downloads, follows, recent updates or newest, on Modrinth and Hangar.
- Click a mod or plugin for its details: downloads, followers, licence, links, and exactly what Install would add (the build, its file and the dependencies it brings).
- Catalog project data is cached for an hour.

## 1.13.2 (2026-11-19)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.13.2.md

- Phantom LAN proxies for Bedrock servers, managed like add-ons (admins): Add-ons -> Server tools -> Phantom installs, starts, stops, restarts and uninstalls a Phantom (jhead/phantom 0.5.4, checked against a pinned SHA-256) that makes a server on a port other than 19132 show up under Friends -> LAN Games. It runs as phantom-<server>.service under the minecraft user, with a proxy port from 19300 up, and opens UDP 19132 and that port in ufw. Install refuses while a Bedrock server holds 19132, and explains why.
- The tab also lists Phantom units made outside the panel that point at the server or hold its port, with their problems, Start / Stop and Remove unit (the unit file only). Removing a server removes its Phantom.

## 1.13.1 (2026-11-19)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.13.1.md

- Java is installed when a server needs it, not with the package: Java moves from Recommends to Suggests, so Bedrock-only hosts get none. Creating, updating or restoring a Java server whose Java isn't installed offers to install the right openjdk-N-jre-headless (admins), then carries on. Servers that were waiting for it are moved onto it.
- Dashboard -> Supporting services -> Java lists each installed Java with the servers on it, the ones servers need, and Install / Remove. Only a Java no server uses can be removed, and only when apt would remove nothing but that JRE.
- Maintenance -> Java shows which Java a server runs on and switches it to another installed one that's new enough.
- Update RedstonePanel from the dashboard (admins): Check for updates and Update now. The upgrade runs as its own systemd unit (redstonepanel upgrade, also usable from a terminal), so it survives the panel's restart; the page reloads on the new version. Game servers keep running.
- Removing a server (Uninstall or Purge) closes its port in ufw and clears its failed state. The dashboard no longer lists failed units whose unit file is gone, such as servers removed by older versions, and clears them.
- Browsers no longer keep running an old app.js after an update (a blank dashboard until Ctrl+F5): the panel's files are sent with Cache-Control: no-cache, and its pages link app.js and the stylesheet with the panel version.

## 1.13.0 (2026-11-19)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.13.0.md

- Find and install mods and plugins from Modrinth (Fabric and Paper) and Hangar (Paper) on the Mods / Plugins tab. Install picks the newest release build for the server's loader and Minecraft version, checks its hash, and installs required dependencies (such as Fabric API) too.
- Updates: the list shows which mods and plugins have a newer build, with Update and Update all (optionally backing up the world first) and Undo for the last update. Jars added by hand are recognised by hash.
- Maintenance -> Update warns about mods and plugins that have no build for the Minecraft version you're moving to.

## 1.12.0 (2026-11-19)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.12.0.md

- Mods, plugins and Bedrock add-ons: a Mods (Fabric), Plugins (Paper) or Add-ons (Bedrock) tab for admins. Drop .jar, .mcpack or .mcaddon files on it; each is checked and put in mods/, plugins/ or the packs folders, and Bedrock packs are enabled for the world. Forge mods, plugins on Fabric, mods on Paper and anything on vanilla are refused with the reason. The list warns about other Minecraft versions and missing dependencies, and can disable or remove each one.
- Files tab: drag and drop uploads, with a progress bar per file.

## 1.11.0 (2026-11-18)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.11.0.md

- Dashboard: with no server selected, the panel shows this machine's CPU, memory, disks and network; each game server's state, uptime, CPU, memory, players, folder and backup sizes and next backup and restart; supporting services (the panel, Java runtimes, screen, ufw, Phantom proxies, timers, HTTPS and cluster certificates, notifications); and a list of what needs attention. Read from /proc, statvfs and systemctl.
- On a cluster master, the dashboard and Cluster page show each node's CPU, memory, disk and servers online, through a new read-only node route, GET /api/host.

## 1.10.0 (2026-11-18)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.10.0.md

- File manager (admins): each server's Files tab browses its folder, edits text files, uploads, downloads, renames, creates folders and deletes. Paths are opened one folder at a time without following symlinks, so nothing outside the server folder can be reached, and new files belong to the folder's owner.

## 1.9.0 (2026-11-18)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.9.0.md

- Java servers can be Paper or Fabric as well as vanilla, on a chosen Minecraft release (the newest by default).
- Each Java server runs the Java its Minecraft version needs, from /usr/lib/jvm; deploy and update name the openjdk package to install when no installed Java is new enough.
- Update can move a Java server to a newer Minecraft release, never an older one, and downloads before stopping the server.
- Vanilla and Paper downloads are checked against their checksums.

## 1.8.1 (2026-11-18)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.8.1.md

- Only admins can uninstall or purge a server. Panel users and trusted-LAN visitors no longer see those buttons, and the API refuses them. Who uninstalled or purged a server is logged.
- User guide: new New Server screenshot.

## 1.8.0 (2026-11-18)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.8.0.md

- Notifications: a Discord or JSON webhook for crashed, started and stopped servers, scheduled restarts and finished or failed backups (Panel Settings > Notifications). Server events come from a systemd drop-in for every minecraft-*.service, so crashes are reported while the panel is stopped. New command: redstonepanel notify-test.
- Scheduled restarts with in-game warnings (Maintenance tab), as redstonepanel-restart@<server>.timer. New command: redstonepanel restart-server NAME.
- The console colours lines by kind.
- Fixed: a crashed Bedrock server was never restarted (screen exits 0 when the game dies). It now starts again after 20 seconds.
- Fixed: a stopped Java server (exit 143) showed as failed.
- Fixed: dark mode's white workspace and unreadable New Server title.

## 1.7.0 (2026-11-18)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.7.0.md

- Scheduled backups. Each server's Backups tab sets a schedule (hourly, every 6 or 12 hours, daily or weekly) run by a systemd timer, redstonepanel-backup@<server>.timer, so backups happen while the panel is stopped. Archives go to /var/backups/redstonepanel/<server>/ (root only); scheduled ones are pruned to the number kept.
- Consistent copies of running servers: save-off/save-all flush/save-on over RCON for Java, save hold/save query/save resume for Bedrock.
- Back up now, download, delete, and restore a backup over the current world (admins). The replaced world is kept as a "Before restore" backup.
- New command: redstonepanel backup NAME.
- Uninstalling a server removes its backup timer and keeps its backups; restoring the server brings the schedule back.

## 1.6.0 (2026-11-18)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.6.0.md

- Restore removed servers. Servers uninstalled with "Keep World" are listed under New Server > Removed servers (and by `redstonepanel removed`); restoring writes a new systemd unit under the same name, keeps the old port unless another server took it, downloads the server binary only if it is missing, then enables and starts it. Also `redstonepanel restore NAME`.
- "Uninstall (Keep World)" now keeps the server binary too, so a restore runs the same game version, and remembers the server's heap size.
- Admins can permanently delete a removed server's files.
- Uninstall refuses names that aren't the panel's own servers.
- Parallel deploys and imports can no longer pick the same server name.

## 1.5.0 (2026-11-17)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.5.0.md

- Clusters: make one panel the master and add other panels as nodes with single-use join codes. The master's Cluster page lists every node's game servers and can start, stop and restart them. A cluster CA issues each member's HTTPS certificate, limited to private addresses and local names. Node tokens only reach game-server listing and control, never users or settings. Encrypted backup and promote recover from a lost master.
- New commands: cluster create/status/join-code/backup/promote, join, leave and cluster-https.
- The header shows the panel's version, also returned by /api/auth/me.
- The server action endpoint only accepts start, stop and restart for the panel's own minecraft* units.

## 1.4.0 (2026-11-17)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.4.0.md

- Bedrock: show why players can't connect. The overview warns when the server rejected its transport (1.26.x accepts only NetherNet), when a NetherNet server started without accepting clients on its TCP port, and when server-udp-ports has a public address in front, which stops that listener on 1.26.63.1. The sidebar marks affected servers.
- Detect Phantom proxies installed as services next to a Bedrock server, whatever their unit name, and flag the two ways they break it: holding UDP 19132 and forwarding to their own port ("Server offline").
- NetherNet servers show their TCP signaling port and UDP gameplay ports.

## 1.3.1 (2026-11-16)

Release notes: release-notes/v1.3.1.md

- Fix Java live chat failing with "not connected" when the RCON password contains symbols such as : = # ! or \, or spaces. Minecraft saves server.properties in Java's escaped format; the panel now reads and writes it the same way, so it uses the password the server uses. It also agrees with Minecraft on trailing spaces and continued lines, which could leave RCON switched off while the panel thought it was on; the panel now turns it back on and restarts the server.
- Saving properties only rewrites the settings that changed.
- Clearer chat errors: a rejected password or RCON not running now says to restart the server (needed after changing rcon.password).

## 1.3.0 (2026-11-14)

- Name each panel to tell several servers apart: the name shows in the sidebar, the browser tab title and on the sign-in page. It defaults to the host name; set it in Panel Settings or with "redstonepanel set-name NAME". "redstonepanel status" shows it.
- Import server backups as .zip, .tar.gz/.tgz or .tar. A backup wrapped in a single folder (e.g. bedrock-server_backup_20261110/) is unpacked from inside it, so the server starts. Archives are unpacked safely (no paths or links outside the server folder) and the edition is detected from the files; non-backups are refused with a clear message.
- Fix the generated systemd units: "RestartSec=20 5" was invalid.

## 1.2.0 (2026-11-14)

- Show the Minecraft version of each server in the sidebar and on the Overview tab; for Java servers also show the Java version it needs and warn when the installed Java is too old.
- Recommend openjdk-25-jre-headless instead of Java 21: current Minecraft Java Edition releases need Java 25.
- Log a clear warning when a deployed or updated Java server needs a newer Java than the one installed.

## 1.1.0 (2026-11-14)

- Fix the World & Maps tab in light mode: its label disappeared on hover and it was styled differently from the other tabs.

## 1.0.0 (2026-11-14)

- Initial package release.
